# Per-box deployment settings for nas-update.sh.
#
# Copy this to ~/.model-manager.env on the NAS and edit it once:
#
#     cp nas-deploy.env.example ~/.model-manager.env
#     chmod 600 ~/.model-manager.env
#
# It deliberately lives outside the repo. Two reasons: this repo is public, and
# the paths and host names of somebody's NAS have no business in it; and
# nas-update.sh replaces its own directory with --delete on every run, so
# anything kept inside the tree is overwritten by the next update.

# --- where the source lands --------------------------------------------------

# Wiped and rewritten on every update, so it must not be a working checkout.
# The script refuses to run if it finds a .git in here, but pick a name that
# cannot collide in the first place.
APP_DIR="$HOME/model-manager-deploy"

# --- where the models are ----------------------------------------------------

# Host paths, bind-mounted into the container. Written relative to $HOME so
# they follow the account, but absolute paths are fine. Note the vendor
# difference if you copy these between boxes: TerraMaster (TOS) uses /Volume1,
# Synology /volume1. Confirm with `df -h`.
MODELS_DIR="$HOME/models"
ARCHIVE_DIR="$HOME/models-archive"

# Database, preview blobs, API token, and download staging.
#
# Put this on the large data volume, never the system partition -- on a NAS
# that root filesystem is often only a few gigabytes. A download is written
# here in full and only then moved to its model folder, so a 12 GB fetch needs
# 12 GB free HERE as well as at the destination. The daemon checks both before
# it starts and refuses in the first second rather than filling a disk.
STATE_DIR="$HOME/model-manager-state"

# --- who the container runs as -----------------------------------------------

# Must own the three directories above, or every write fails with a permission
# error. Check with:  ls -n /VolumeN/<share>
# Root-owned NAS volumes want 0:0.
CONTAINER_USER="0:0"

# --- how it is reached -------------------------------------------------------

# host:container. Change the left side to move it off 8737 on the NAS.
PORT="8737:8737"

# Every host name or address the web UI is opened by, space separated.
#
# The daemon rejects any Host header it was not told about. That is the
# DNS-rebinding defence and it cannot know its own names, so a missing entry
# here looks exactly like the server being down -- the connection succeeds and
# every request is refused. Include the short name, any .local or tailnet name,
# and the bare IP if you ever type one.
ALLOW_HOSTS="<nas-hostname> <nas-hostname>.local <lan-ip> <tailnet-ip>"

# --- optional ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Path to the docker binary. Only needed if it is somewhere the script does not
# already look. It searches the PATH, the usual TOS and Synology app locations,
# and finally a login shell -- which matters because a remote `ssh host "sh
# nas-update.sh"` is not a login shell and does not inherit the vendor's PATH.
#DOCKER="/Volume1/@apps/DockerEngine/dockerd/bin/docker"

# API keys, only needed for models gated behind a login. Browsing and public
# downloads work without them.
#CIVITAI_API_KEY=""
#HF_TOKEN=""

# GitHub token, only if this repo is ever made private. The tarball fetch is
# anonymous while it is public.
#TOKEN_FILE="$HOME/.gh-token"

# Which Civitai catalogue to search. civitai.com is the main site, .red the
# adult split, .green the safe-for-work one.
#MM_CIVITAI_API="https://civitai.com/api/v1"

# How often the archive watchlist is checked for new versions. 0 disables the
# timer and leaves the endpoint, for intake without the daemon acting on its own.
#ARCHIVE_INTERVAL="6h"
